From e317869a2581e57b8dd61dec94b7f31f153b15b5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: colneech-dev <colneech@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 00:35:54 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] placer: congestion awareness against real routing
 capacity (steps 1-3)

Three gaps, found by reading the source rather than assuming:

1. NOTHING in the flow computed routing CAPACITY. The RUDY demand
   estimate in CutSpreader could only be compared against an absolute
   hand-tuned constant (NEXTPNR_WIRE_DEMAND), which does not transfer
   between designs or devices. Adds a per-tile capacity map built from
   ctx->getWires(), using the SAME wire-location rule as router2s
   setup_wires() so placer demand and router overuse refer to the same
   tile by construction rather than by coincidence.

2. Congestion is now expressible as demand/capacity (NEXTPNR_CONG_RATIO),
   a dimensionless ratio where 1.0 means the estimate expects to use
   exactly the routing a tile has.

3. Congestion could previously only TRIGGER spreading, never steer it:
   once a region was flagged, the cut still balanced raw cell counts and
   was free to leave half the cells on the hotspot. Congestion now
   tightens the region density target (cell-area inflation, expressed in
   the units this spreader already uses) and weights the cut objective so
   the balance point moves cells off the congested side
   (NEXTPNR_CONG_SPREAD).

Corrects one thing I had assumed: build_wire_demand() is NOT computed
once -- CutSpreader is constructed per HeAP iteration, so the demand map
already tracked the placement. Only the capacity denominator was missing.

Both knobs default off and multiply by exactly 1.0 when off, so an
unflagged run is unchanged.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
